## 🎯 Recap – What you end up with

| Category | Kept | Removed |
|----------|------|---------|
| **Mail provider** | `GmailProvider` only | `OutlookProvider` & all Outlook routes |
| **Calendar** | Google‑Calendar utils only | Microsoft‑Calendar utils |
| **AI** | All `utils/ai/*` you need (summarise, draft, persona, etc.) | None of the AI code is removed (they’re lightweight) |
| **Background queue** | No‑op stub (`utils/upstash.ts`) | QStash library, all `/api/*/qstash*` routes |
| **Analytics** | No‑op stub (`utils/noopPosthog.ts`) | PostHog provider, hooks, UI components |
| **Billing / Premium** | None | Stripe webhooks, `ee/` folder, price‑config, premium‑filter clauses |
| **Transactional mail** | None (optional simple SMTP wrapper if you ever need it) | Resend SDK, digest/summary API endpoints |
| **Database** | `EmailAccount`, `EmailMessage`, `User`, `CalendarEvent` (if you use it) | Tables that only served Stripe/Resend (e.g. `Premium`, `StripeCustomer`) – optional to drop |

You now have a **tiny, focused server** that:

* Authenticates the user via Google OAuth.
* Pulls messages from Gmail (with pagination, date filters, “‑label:DRAFT”).
* Stores them locally in a Prisma‑backed `EmailMessage` table.
* Lets you call any of the AI helpers you kept.

All the heavyweight, unrelated parts are gone, so start‑up is fast, the Docker image (or Vercel deployment) is small, and you only need a handful of environment variables.

If you later decide you want to re‑introduce a feature (e.g. a queue or analytics), you can simply add the original file back – the rest of the code remains compatible.
